<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Walter von Cronberg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1479–<a href="/years/1543">1543</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Walter von Cronberg was German Master of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> at Mergentheim. After Grand Master Albert secularised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1525, the emperor made him administrator of the grand mastership in 1527, and his successors combined the two offices, ruling the order's remaining lands from Mergentheim in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/walter-von-cronberg.jpg" alt="Walter von Cronberg" width="320" height="487" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WalthervonCronberg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Walter von Cronberg came from a rather poor family of knights from Kronberg Castle near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>. He joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> in 1497, was a tax collector in the Komturei of Mergentheim from 1499 and became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/komtur">Komtur</a> of Frankfurt in 1504. Under Grand Master Albert of Brandenburg-Ansbach he served as the order's legate to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-i-the-old">Sigismund I the Old</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, and in 1517 he founded the Brotherhood of St. Sebastian. In 1526 he was chosen Deutschmeister, master of the order's German branch.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When Albert converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> and was excommunicated, von Cronberg declared himself the next Grand Master. He based his claim on a 14th-century statute of Werner von Orseln, which provided that if the Grand Master was absent, the master of one of the other branches would take the position. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolter-von-plettenberg">Wolter von Plettenberg</a>, master of the Livonian branch, also claimed the office. Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> averted the conflict in 1527 by deciding in von Cronberg's favour and naming him Administrator of the Office of Grand Master.</p><p>At the same time the Grand Master's claim to Albert's Duchy of Prussia was renewed. Because no control could be exercised there, the Grand Master's seat moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a> to Mergentheim, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a>, which had been the seat of the Deutschmeister in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>From 1530 von Cronberg devoted himself to preserving the Catholic character of the order. He could not stop its further secularisation within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, as more and more knights broke their oaths by turning Protestant or by refusing obedience to the Catholic Grand Masters. He died on 4 April 1543, having led the order from 1527, and is counted as its 38th Grand Master.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Walter von Cronberg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Holy Roman Empire and the Catholic Church banned Albert for his Protestantism, and the Teutonic Knights under <b>Walter von Cronberg</b> went on claiming Prussia without success.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-prussia">Duchy of Prussia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>When Grand Master Albert secularised Prussia in 1525, the order's German master, <b>Walter von Cronberg</b>, who resided at Mergentheim in Franconia, was made administrator of the grand mastership in 1527, and his successors combined both offices as Hoch- und Deutschmeister.</blockquote><a href="/state/teutonic-order-mergentheim">Teutonic Order Mergentheim</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kronberg im Taunus, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bad-mergentheim">Bad Mergentheim</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.18,8.51&amp;year=1520" aria-label="Kronberg im Taunus on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Walter von Cronberg"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5464 5806h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5464 5806h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.18,8.51&amp;year=1520">What was here in 1520? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
